00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.990 align:start position:0%
 
the results are in for germany's  national elections and here at dw news  based in berlin we'll explain how this  might impact the asia region  we do not yet know who the next  chancellor will be  that will take time as the political  parties now negotiate  to form a coalition government  one thing to keep in mind the new  chancellor and the new foreign minister  tend to come from different parties here  are where things stand the center-left  social democrats have the tiniest of  leads with about 26 percent of the vote  the conservative cdu csu merkel's party  comes second either one could form a  coalition government with other  supporting parties  how is asia reacting to this news we  start with dw's amrita tima in india and  journalist fabian kreshmer in china  fabian it might be too soon for chinese  leadership to say anything just yet but  any early headlines from state media and  do we know what beijing most wants in  the post-miracle world  yes  angela merkel has visited china a dozen  times and at the end of her  chancellorship was actually covered  really with a lot of nostalgia by the  state media here and that should not  surprise because angela merkel is  basically um yeah the best possible  outcome also for the next government  basically her china policy was um in  beijing's eyes very  pragmatic and in a first reaction the  spokesperson of the foreign minister  ministry said that um we expect a  continuation of the pragmatic  china policy also from the next  government however um there's also a lot  of criticism for merkel's china policy  because it's quite soft and she was  rather subdued with her criticism  towards human rights what actually  beijing fears the most is that germany  will seek  tighter cooperation with america in  their china policy but if you look at  the program  of the social democrats and also the  christian democrats that is not really  likely to happen the biggest actually  criticism towards china comes from the  green party  right  amerita how is delhi watching these  results any reactions from local media  you know the local media has been  watching these elections but i have to  say the biggest and only election party  in town was at the german embassy it  wasn't the usual big bash that you have  on election night that's because of the  corona situation the numbers were kept  fairly restricted but the headlines  coming out of these elections even now  are the departure of angela merkel from  the german political stage and that's  because you know uh angela merkel  invested a huge deal in building indoor  german relations over the past 16 years  and also enjoyed a very good rapport  with prime minister narendra modi and  she made india a priority in strategic  terms so of course people are asking the  question what will her departure mean  and the media is also worried about the  fact it was such a close uh election  result that there will be uncertainty  over the coming months as a coalition  government is formed but broadly as far  beyond said people are expecting a great  deal of continuity on the broader policy  issues between india and germany for  example security how to deal with the  pandemic climate change and so on but  some changes are expected because of the  presence of the two smaller parties in  any governing coalition be there it led  by a and the spd or by the cdu that is  the greens and the business friendly ftp  now the ftp of course uh has very strong  increase in business and trade and  investment and they could set their own  accents uh and for india you know it  could be kind of bring some changes as  well as the green party because the  green party may raise issues like human  rights concerns and the crackdown on  descent and that could ruffle some  feathers within the establishment here  thank you amrita and fabian for joining  us  what will determine how germany engages  with asia depends on the kind of  coalition government the political  parties build the two most viable  options at the moment are either the  social democrats teaming up with the  greens and the libertarian free  democrats also known as the fdp or  merkel's christian democrats who came in  a tight second team me up with the same  the greens and the fdp  [Applause]  euphoria  and disappointment  as the party faithful reacted to the  numbers their bosses gathered face to  face to kick off the next fight who will  rule together  the social democrats olaf schultz moved  first  i will say that there are a few parties  who have increased their vote the spd  the greens and the fdp and the others  have not increased their share i think  that's a message  followed by this question to merkel's  choice set the tone  the question i have to ask can you  remain party leader  we don't have all the results we're  waiting for them  we've recognized today that only someone  who has a majority in the german  bundestag will become chancellor  he will need the greens and the fdp  their leader started the mind games  the greens and fdp should talk to each  other first then we can structure what  comes next  we've already made it clear in the last  few days robert habeck and i that this  logic that one calls all the others  wouldn't help the revival that we need  in this country therefore it's more than  sensible that different parties talk  with each other in different  constellations  schultz sensed the danger but seemed  confident that reaching a deal would be  easy  i have always said that there are lots  of overlaps with my neighbor but just  the two of us won't be enough it'll have  to be three parties i think we'll settle  this fairly quickly  despite the disastrous result lashit  refused to give up hope  the party in first was not always the  one that provided the chancellor  the great task of the democratic parties  is to see where there is the greatest  common ground  sounds simple enough but in germany's  multi-party system anything but  joining us in the studio is dw's chief  international editor richard walker  richard when it comes to foreign policy  is china the biggest challenge for this  next government  yeah melissa i i think you can say  pretty clearly that it is uh at the very  top of the list and that doesn't make  germany unique after all you know the  entirety of the west is really uh being  confronted and this has become into  sharpest relief in the last couple of  years really confronted with this  challenge of of a rising superpower in  china that's increasingly forthright in  its application of authoritarian power  both within its borders uh and outside  them um and particularly you know the  united states is facing the challenge of  trying to manage its intensifying  competition  with china in a way that doesn't lead to  uh the risk of of ending up in a hot war  which is which is something that many  analysts are really worried about  now for germany  this is a particularly acute situation  because germany's business ties with  china are really massive and that is  something that has emerged out of the  angela merkel eras we just heard from  fabian earlier there in beijing angela  merkel has had a very pro-engagement  policy  for the last 10 or 12 years with china  in particular and this has led to a huge  flourishing of business ties between  germany and china and germany different  from some countries in the west it  doesn't just import a lot of goods from  china it exports enormous amounts of  goods to china so some see even an  economic dependency on china so this is  a massive challenge to negotiate this  sort of tightrope walk that  that many see going ahead  now the greens have been vocal about  china's human rights record and have  pushed for a tougher position against  beijing they're very likely to be part  of any coalition government so how much  of an influence will they have on  germany's foreign policy in regards to  china  well it depends on the exact  constellation of the government as we've  just heard earlier  now we're entering this period of horse  trading between the between the parties  trying to come up with a coalition  there's a very good chance that the  greens could end up with the foreign  ministry so essentially the ministries  get dished out between the parties as  part of the coalition arrangements  the other alternative is that the free  democrats the liberal party could get  the foreign ministry and they're also  very outspoken on human rights so it's a  likely outcome of this election that  whoever is foreign minister will be have  a much stronger position on human rights  that could definitely lead to more  friction with china we heard from fabian  earlier that the spokesperson of the  foreign ministry already saying that  they seek a pragmatic relationship with  china that's the sort of thing that they  don't see as pragmatic western countries  criticizing them over their human rights  record so it could be interesting times  ahead but we should stress that in a  coalition you know no one party can  really dominate the agenda and remember  that the leading party will either be  the conservatives or the social  democrats we can expect from both of  those parties a bit more of the  continuity that we've of the approach  that broadly speaking merkel has had got  it so there will be change but perhaps  nothing too big now germany has a big  influence on eu foreign policy and of  course the eu recently released its new  indo-pacific strategy how might these  election results influence that policy  well i think you you can certainly see  whether it's the free democrats or the  greens you can see either of these two  parties wanting to take a more active  role really in shaping not just within  the eu but also uh within nato for  instance the free democrats they say  that they want to see a a clearer  strategy i'm reading from their policy  paper here  in dealing with china  cooperation with nato and democratic  partner states in asia  now this is kind of unfinished business  really from from from the end of the  angela merkel government i was just with  haiku master the outgoing foreign  minister in the united states for the  u.n general assembly talks and he was  saying to me as well that he really  wants to see the european union and the  americans and partners in the region  coming up with a much more unified  strategy of of how to deal with this  region the indo-pacific so in a way that  that is a kind of an issue that bubbled  up towards the end of the merkle  government and is now going to see new  follow-through i think either of those  parties going to be very engaged in  those debates in the years ahead richard  thank you so much for joining us  and that's it for the show there is  other news and analysis from the region  as always on our website dw.com forward  slash asia and you can follow us on  facebook and twitter as well thanks for  joining and we'll see you tomorrow  goodbye  you

